WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:05.657
So I was working on a project where we had a performance problem with a website.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:13.441
And we looked at it, and it was really kind of strange because we would see these strange unexplained spikes in traffic,

00:00:13.441 --> 00:00:16.509
and yet the number of sessions was approximately the same.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:24.709
So we looked at the logs and we realized there wasn't just more users, it was some users were consuming an insane amount of resources.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.269
We traced it down to what we were calling "bad bots."

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:35.469
And so for this presentation, I want to explain how we fixed this problem and how you could potentially address this same problem.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.349
So first, what do I mean by a bad bot?

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:39.979
Let's look at the extremes.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.849
First we have the "good" bots.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:45.850
Those are the the Googles, the Bings, the DuckDuckGo, etc.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:50.200
Those are ones that don't strain your resources too much.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.580
And they also respect the rules of the road, as it were [ed: like valid user agent in HTTP request].

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.885
And then we have at the other extreme, which I'm not going to cover in this particular presentation,

00:00:57.885 --> 00:01:01.479
those that represent a security problem--what I call "evil" bots.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:07.150
And those are bots that are really trying to compromise the admin login or something like that.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:10.299
Those are ones that I'm not going to refer to in this specific one.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.800
I'm more interested in the ones that are really badly behaved.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:17.605
In other words, they're demanding more resources and how can you address them.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.530
I'm going to cover this in three different parts.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:25.629
The first part is kind of a general protection strategy that you can use for your website.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:31.420
The second, which is directly to this point, is how can you detect bad behavior and what to do about it.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.969
And then the third is kind of what you should be aware of with this--some of the caveats for this solution.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:42.469
So first, I understand this diagrams a little bit busy, so work with me on this one.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:50.314
This is a three-tier architecture where you have your web [server] being accessed by different users and bots,

00:01:50.314 --> 00:01:55.400
which is-- and skipping over just for a moment --accessing your web servers.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:58.500
That goes to an application server and then to your back-end database.

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.490
That is the original topology.

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:07.150
We're going to introduce some new components in this and I'm going to explain them one-by-one, starting from the left and heading over.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.580
So from the users, we head over to a proxy, which is a reverse proxy.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:12.590
So what is that?

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:19.059
A reverse proxy is your forward-facing sort of interface to the Internet.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:24.879
Normally your web server would be outward facing and that way the users and bots, etc.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.669
know the IP addresses of those particular servers.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.580
What you do is you put a reverse proxy in front of those and that becomes the public face of it.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:34.960
So why do you do that?

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:36.789
Well, there's a couple of different reasons.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:42.133
One is it allows you to filter traffic; so that way, if there's malicious traffic,

00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:50.830
you can pull that off, or you can also introduce other components very easily, like a CDN, which is a content delivery network.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.379
A content delivery network basically is a set of servers located throughout the world

00:02:56.379 --> 00:03:04.925
which capture the static sort of resources from your web servers, cache them, and then deliver them to users.

00:03:04.925 --> 00:03:07.389
That takes off some of the load from these back-end servers.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.609
While you're there, another handy component is a load balancer.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:18.429
And that allows you to be able to distribute the load to your back-end servers across multiple servers.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.850
And so, for example, if it's Black Friday and you've got a lot of traffic you anticipate,

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:29.800
you can add additional servers to handle that additional load, and then when the event's over, you can go ahead and decommission them.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:34.491
Okay, so that was the big picture for the beginning of the general protection,

00:03:34.491 --> 00:03:40.840
where in the proxy we can add filtering to be able to handle some of these potential evil or bad bots.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:49.206
But what I want to really lead to is, is this star of the show, that is a web application firewall (WAF).

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:53.110
That is different from a firewall that you might traditionally think of in the Linux sense

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:58.689
of having a firewall where you're blocking by IP address or service to your server.

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:04.949
A web application firewall is programable, and it has a number of rules.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:12.249
In fact, typically it comes with thousands of predefined rules that can detect this sort of invalid access you want to prevent.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:20.019
But here's the interesting part: You can also program it dynamically and that I could use to detect this bad behavior.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:22.359
So what do I mean by bad behavior?

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:24.970
Let's go through several examples to explain what I mean.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.191
So if you are looking through your logs and you're seeing accesses, let's say for a user,

00:04:30.191 --> 00:04:35.172
you might see something where they access the page within a given interval of four pages

00:04:35.172 --> 00:04:38.444
and then they're going to pause,-- to be able to read that.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.679
They might access another page during the next interval and so on.

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:50.304
The point being is, is that normal user behavior is: read some pages, take a pause, read some more pages, etc., right?

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:52.220
A good bot.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:57.135
What it does is kind of similar to this pattern where it might ask for a page,

00:04:57.135 --> 00:05:04.876
ask for a page, and then if your server returns an HTTP return code of 429 [Too Many Requests], it pauses.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:10.625
So, for example, you could say 429 [Too Many Requests] HTTP code, you know, let's wait 30 seconds.

00:05:10.625 --> 00:05:15.160
And it will respect that and thus it will stop requesting pages and then resume.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:28.129
The bad bots, on the other hand, they will request page after page after page, and they will not respect the fact that you've entered a 429.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:36.780
And evil [bot], which I haven't covered in this case, they will request 100 pages, 100 pages--as much as your server can literally withstand

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:43.260
to be able to consume that sort of [content] from your pages, and it won't respect that.

00:05:43.499 --> 00:05:51.283
So if we could detect this particular case, what we do is we install a monitor here.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:06:00.690
And this monitor will capture the logs from your different web servers looking for this particular pattern.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:07.476
And if it detects what we are calling this "bad bot", one that is not respecting a 429 [Too Many Requests],

00:06:07.476 --> 00:06:11.300
it can then create a dynamic rule.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:18.199
For example, to block IP traffic for, say, another 30 minutes.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:27.889
That way, typically your bad bots, if they get too many dropped requests, they'll simply move on and you won't have a problem from there.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:35.514
This will smooth out those top spikes and be able to give you much less resource consumption.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:45.270
In fact, in our tests we found that typical bad bot activity reduction was 15 to 45% of your resources.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:51.629
So not only do you save on the amount of resources you're consuming, but your users ultimately get faster performance.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:54.119
Okay, some caveats before I wrap up.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.350
First and foremost is this is just **a** solution.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:03.720
There are other ways you can approach the same problem, so I recommend that you iterate on this solution.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:05.850
This is a good starting point.

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:11.369
Also, keep in mind that if you're using a reverse proxy, there is some overhead.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:14.680
You can see that I'm jumping from one server to the next.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:23.040
Those additional hops can introduce additional latency that can cause you time that you might not have if you were connecting directly.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:27.120
But it isn't that much-- for a typical [case], on the order of, say, 20 milliseconds.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:35.276
And probably, finally, the most important point is, is keep in mind that if your reverse proxy is being provided by a third party,

00:07:35.276 --> 00:07:37.883
you really need to be able to trust them.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:45.269
Because that proxy is going to see the IP addresses and HTTP request headers that go all back to your servers.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:50.049
So that means you need to trust that they are going to be responsible about that privacy

00:07:50.049 --> 00:07:59.610
and also that they are taking security steps to make sure that the data they are capturing is not going to be compromised in some sort of way.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:01.870
Thanks for watching.

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:04.860
Before you leave, hey, don't forget to hit like and subscribe!

